# Whale Singer

## Lore

Whale Singers can be humans, simvan, dog boys, sea titans, amphibs, naut''yll, Lemurians and other races, aquatic or not - though the vast majority are dolphins, porpoises, whales and pneuma-biforms.

Those who are human or humanoid are usually treated with respect by sailors and sea communities. Passage on a ship typically costs a Whale Singer 50 to 80 percent less than it would anyone else, especially where trouble with sea monsters is expected or the captain is feeling unlucky, and some coastal communities discount meals, drinks and lodging by 10 to 25 percent. Food, travel and basic needs are often traded for the Singer''s help instead. Ocean wizards and sea druids get some of the same treatment, to a lesser degree.

## GM Notes

Only the first Whale Singer ability - recognising another of their kind - is common to every member. The rest are available to those who are not cetaceans or pneuma-biforms, which is what makes the class worth taking for a human.

A human or surface-dwelling D-bee not adapted to living underwater cannot sing a spellsong normally: it must be performed as a ritual, underwater, taking 1D6x10 minutes before the magic activates, and it reaches only a tenth of its normal range. Most aquatic D-bees manage half range. At the G.M.''s discretion, other races that communicate like whales and dolphins might manage full range.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'whale-singer');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'whale-singer';
SELECT count(*) AS stub_gear FROM gear WHERE slug IN ('scaling-knife', 'fishing-pole', 'fishing-hooks-and-lures', 'wet-suit', 'scuba-gear');

